TAM-EDIT

tam-edit is a terminal text editor written in C11 that links only libtam
(https://github.com/greenermoose/libtam) and the C library. It reproduces
micro (https://github.com/micro-editor/micro): it began as a fork of
micro, and micro's behaviour, documentation and runtime files remain its
reference.

The repository started fresh on 2026-11-18 from the C11 editor at 1.0.4,
without micro's Git history. The old history, including the Go version
of tam-edit on its master branch, is archived read-only at
greenermoose/tam-edit-micro-fork
(https://github.com/greenermoose/tam-edit-micro-fork). Commit hashes in
work/history and in the work records before W3 refer to that archive.

Report tam-edit bugs here, at greenermoose/tam-edit
(https://github.com/greenermoose/tam-edit), not upstream. The Upstream
section below maps micro's source and documentation to the features
being ported.

Follows the tam-idol 0.2.0 standard.


BRANCHES AND STATUS

  dev    development work; agents push it freely
  main   tam-edit once it is ready; created and moved only on Fred's
         word

ROADMAP.md holds the phase plan and the milestone definitions.

Version 1.0.3 (Milestone 5) completes the Phase 7 parity gate: full parity
audit (docs/PARITY_AUDIT.md), 57 ported VSCode/micro buffer corpus tests,
large-file/long-line/many-cursor/many-split stress tests, ASan+UBSan fuzz
harnesses (make fuzz), Section 8 performance verification, man page
(docs/tam-edit.1) and desktop entry (data/tam-edit.desktop), and libc-only
linkage.


VERSIONING

tam-edit follows semantic versioning, and while it is below 1.0.0 the
minor number names a milestone: 0.1.0 is the first daily-usable editor,
0.2.0 is views and configuration, and 1.0.0 is reserved for the parity gate
with micro. VERSION at the repository root is the only source (read by the Makefile); tam-edit --version
prints it next to the libtam version. The milestone table and the bump
rules are in ROADMAP.md (Milestones and Versions).


WHAT TAM-EDIT CHANGES FROM MICRO

- The binary and command name are tam-edit.
- In the Go reference (archived), configuration comes from
  TAM_EDIT_CONFIG_HOME, then $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/tam-edit, then
  ~/.config/tam-edit. It never falls back to an existing micro
  configuration directory.
- The Go reference accepts TAM_EDIT_TRUECOLOR alongside MICRO_TRUECOLOR,
  which still works because it describes the terminal rather than the
  configuration.
- Go-reference Lua plugins may import tam-edit/* as well as micro/*.
- The reference toolchain is pinned in mise.toml; Go is optional for C11
  work.
- The C11 editor is C only and leaves out Go, tcell, Lua plugins,
  the plugin manager and the embedded terminal, which are v1 non-goals in
  ROADMAP.md.


BUILDING

Requires a C11 compiler, make, pkg-config and libtam 0.7.0 or later.

  make                 # build/tam-edit
  make test            # unit tests
  make check-deps      # fail if anything other than libc is linked
  make install PREFIX=$HOME/.local/dev
  make uninstall

Run it as tam-edit FILE. PREFIX defaults to $HOME/.local/dev and the
install targets respect DESTDIR. If libtam is outside pkg-config's search
path, add its pkgconfig directory to PKG_CONFIG_PATH.

The Go editor is no longer in this repository; its source and tests are
on master in the archive. Syntax definitions, colorschemes, and help
pages remain in runtime/ alongside the embedded tables in data/.


